// MaxAckPending mirrors jetstream.ConsumerConfig.MaxAckPending: the
// server-side cap on deliveries outstanding un-acked across ALL replicas
// sharing the durable; zero uses the server default (1000). Distinct
// from MaxMessages below, which caps only this process's client-side
// buffer.
MaxAckPending int

// BackOff mirrors jetstream.ConsumerConfig.BackOff: the SERVER-side
// redelivery ladder, one delay per redelivery, applied in place of
// AckWait. It is mutually exclusive with Retry — setting both is a Start
// error, not a warning — because the two do not compose: the server
// stretches a handler's NakWithDelay by the BackOff increments, so
// redeliveries land on neither schedule and the configured ladder becomes
// dead config that reads as authoritative. That is ENT-1535's root
// finding, and [Retry] exists to make the combination unrepresentable.
//
// Leave it nil and use Retry for new consumers. It is here so a consumer
// that genuinely wants the server ladder can say so, and so the choice is
// visible in one place.
BackOff []time.Duration

// Retry is the library-owned retry mechanism: one redelivery ladder, the
// floor-age circuit breaker, and dead-letter capture. When set, [Start]
// runs an ack-floor poll for the breaker, clears any server-side BackOff
// left on the durable, and [Process] dead-letters an undecodable payload
// instead of Terming it. Its MaxDeliver must equal EffectiveMaxDeliver().
//
// Handlers reach it through the closure they build, and call
// [Retry.Settle] as their one disposition for a failed delivery. Nil
// leaves disposition entirely to the handler, as before.
Retry *Retry

